          "text": "Submitted. Summary of the findings in `tenacity/__init__.py` (synchronous `Retrying`):\n\n1. **after on accepted success — false.** In `_post_retry_check_actions`, when the retry predicate returns false (no explicit TryAgain, no retry), the code queues `rs.outcome.result()` and returns immediately; the `self.after` action is only added in the retry path below that early return.\n\n2. **wait computed before stop — true.** The retry path queues `_run_wait`, then `_run_stop`, then `_post_stop_check_actions`, and `iter` executes actions in queued order, so `upcoming_sleep` is computed before `stop` is evaluated.\n\n3. **before_sleep on the stopped attempt — false.** In `_post_stop_check_actions`, when `stop_run_result` is true, it queues the error callback/`exc_check` and returns; the `before_sleep` action is only queued after that, in the continuing path.\n\n4. **retry_error_callback replaces exhaustion error — true.** When stopped and a `retry_error_callback` is configured, it is queued instead of `exc_check` (which would raise `retry_error_cls`), and its return value becomes `do`, which `Retrying.__call__` returns directly.",
